      "refersTo": "actions-menu",
      "description": "The floating ⋯ menu; the only shadowed layer; destructive items last.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-menu\" role=\"menu\"><button class=\"ds-menu-item\" role=\"menuitem\">Modifier</button><hr class=\"ds-menu-sep\"><button class=\"ds-menu-item ds-danger\" role=\"menuitem\">Supprimer</button></div>",
      "css": ".ds-menu{display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;min-width:8rem;padding:4px;border-radius:10px;background:var(--popover,oklch(1 0 0));color:var(--popover-foreground,oklch(0.22 0.012 255));box-shadow:0 4px 6px -1px rgb(0 0 0/.1),0 2px 4px -2px rgb(0 0 0/.1),0 0 0 1px color-mix(in oklch,var(--foreground,oklch(0.22 0.012 255)) 10%,transparent);font:400 .875rem \"Atkinson Hyperlegible Next\",sans-serif}.ds-menu-item{padding:6px 8px;border:0;border-radius:8px;background:transparent;color:inherit;text-align:left;font:inherit;cursor:pointer;outline:none}.ds-menu-item:hover,.ds-menu-item:focus-visible{background:var(--accent,oklch(0.95 0.025 162));color:var(--accent-foreground,oklch(0.34 0.08 162))}.ds-menu-sep{margin:4px -4px;border:0;border-top:1px solid var(--border,oklch(0.91 0.006 255))}.ds-danger{color:var(--destructive,oklch(0.55 0.2 27))}.ds-danger:hover,.ds-danger:focus-visible{background:color-mix(in oklch,var(--destructive,oklch(0.55 0.2 27)) 10%,transparent);color:var(--destructive,oklch(0.55 0.2 27))}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Night Map and the Enamel Plate",
    "overview": "Correspondance draws a personal checklist as a metro map. Every section is a line, numbered in a round badge and painted in one of six line colours handed out by position; every item is a station on that line's vertical track. Checking a station fills its ring and draws the leg that leaves it in the line colour, so a section's progress is the stretch of track already travelled. The count beside the line's name (\"2/4\") is the only other progress signal; there is no bar.\n\nThe ground is signage: white enamel by day, the blue-black of a night map by night. Both themes are first-class and every token is defined for both. Colour comes almost entirely from the lines; the surrounding interface is neutral ink on a neutral ground, with line 12's green doubling as the primary action, focus and brand colour. There are no cards: lines sit directly on the ground, laid out as a map in one column on phones and two from 1024px.\n\nOne face, Atkinson Hyperlegible Next, carries every level. Hierarchy comes from weight and size alone. Secondary actions stay hidden until hover or keyboard focus (always visible on coarse pointers), and things not yet made (a next station, a new line) are drawn as dashed outlines of the real thing.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Sections are lines, items are stations, progress is travelled track.",
      "Six line colours assigned by position; line 12 green leads and is also primary.",
      "Flat signage on a white or night-map ground; no cards, no shadows at rest.",
      "One legible sans at every level; rank by weight and size.",
      "Not-yet-made things are dashed outlines with a plus."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Line Colour Rule",
        "body": "Line colours belong to lines. A colour is assigned by a section's position, never chosen per section, and it appears only on that line's badge, track and stations. Chrome around the map stays neutral, except Line 12 Green in its primary role.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Faint Track Rule",
        "body": "A track ahead is its line colour at 25% (30% for yellow and sky, which are lighter); travelled track is the full colour. Never a grey track.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Browser Wears the Palette Rule",
        "body": "Caret, native accent, selection and scrollbar take the tokens (`caret-color` and `accent-color` green, selection a 28% green mix, scrollbar thumb Field Edge).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Face Rule",
        "body": "Atkinson Hyperlegible Next at every level. Rank by weight (400 / 500 / 700) and size from the shared `sm | md | lg | xl` scale.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Caps Label Rule",
        "body": "No uppercase labels, eyebrows or small-caps section tags. A line's identity is its badge and its name. Wide tracking is reserved for the centred PIN field.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Map Is Flat Rule",
        "body": "Nothing on the map is lifted. A shadow means the element floats above the map and will close.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do assign line colours with `lineColorAt(index)` in the fixed order green, blue, magenta, orange, yellow, sky.",
      "Do show progress as travelled track plus a tabular count (\"2/4\"); the track is the progress indicator.",
      "Do draw anything not yet created as a dashed outline of the real thing with a plus (next station, open line).",
      "Do define every new colour for both the signage-white and night-map themes, with an ink for any line-coloured fill.",
      "Do keep secondary actions hidden until hover or focus, and always visible on touch.",
      "Do keep rows at least 44px tall and keep the station column aligned with the track.",
      "Do follow `docs/best-practices.md` for how tokens reach components (token classes only, cva variants typed against `tokens.ts`)."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't put lines in cards or panels; lines sit on the ground.",
      "Don't use a progress bar, percentage ring or a generic square checkbox on a line; stations are rings.",
      "Don't use a line colour for chrome, or pick a line's colour by hand.",
      "Don't add a second typeface, a serif, or uppercase labels and eyebrows.",
      "Don't add shadows to anything on the map; shadows are for floating, dismissable layers.",
      "Don't animate a leg for longer than its two 100ms halves, or at all under reduced motion.",
      "Don't reproduce the RATP logo, name or official line numbering artwork."
    ]
  }
}
